.TH CCOL_R_MEMPOOL_ALLOC_ENTRY 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_r_mempool_alloc_entry \- allocate an entry from the ranged pool
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cmempool.h>

void *ccol_r_mempool_alloc_entry(ccol_r_mempool *rmp, size_t size);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Allocates memory of the requested size from the best sub-pool. The size
is rounded up to the nearest power-of-2 pool, and when that pool is
empty the larger pools are tried, except under
ccol_fallback_at_first_exhaustion, where that pool takes the memory
from the heap instead. What happens on exhaustion depends on the
policy; see ccol_r_mempool_create(3).
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
rmp   Ranged memory pool to allocate from
size  Size in bytes to allocate. It must be > 0 and <= largest_size
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a pointer to the allocated entry on success
Returns NULL on failure, which includes a size of 0 or above
largest_size
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
O(1) in the common case, O(number_of_pools) in the worst case
The memory that comes back is uninitialized; use
ccol_r_mempool_calloc_entry() for zeroed memory
The pointer that comes back is aligned for any object type, as with
malloc(), so any object that you can keep in heap memory can go into a
pool entry
The size is rounded up to the next power-of-2 pool size
When the best pool is empty, larger and larger pools are tried, unless
the policy is ccol_fallback_at_first_exhaustion
Thread-safe if the pool was created with single_threaded=false
The caller must free the entry with ccol_r_mempool_free_entry()
It asserts if rmp is NULL
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_r_mempool_calloc_entry (3),
.BR ccol_r_mempool_realloc_entry (3),
.BR ccol_r_mempool_free_entry (3)
